;
FILE TRANSFERS

A - Taking software home
B - Sending something to the sysop

:A
TAKING SOFTWARE HOME

A file transfer involves two programs: one sending and one receiving. Entering
the command at the RCP/M end is only half the job. Your terminal program must
be ready to receive the file.

The supplied download command is YSEND, which uses YMODEM. A small text file
makes a convenient first test:

    YSEND A0:WELCOME.TXT

When the RCP/M is ready to send, select YMODEM receive in your terminal,
unless it has already started receiving automatically. Choose your local
download directory according to the terminal's instructions. Wait for the
transfer to complete and for the CP/M prompt to return.

To take a whole library home, for example:

    YSEND P0:SIGMV011.LBR

Make sure the file is on the currently mounted disk. A library is a binary
file: do not run it through a terminal's text capture or character-set
conversion.

If the RCP/M appears to be doing nothing after you start a transfer, it may
simply be waiting for the receiving end. Check that you've selected the
corresponding protocol and the right transfer direction. If a transfer fails,
use the terminal's transfer-cancel facility and follow the remote program's
prompts before trying again.

The YMODEM download service can send files from readable directories even when
those files are not approved for execution on the board. The two permissions
are quite different.

:B
SENDING SOMETHING TO THE SYSOP

Perhaps you've found a CP/M program missing from the collection, or have
written a utility of your own. The sysop will generally appreciate a useful
contribution, particularly if it comes with instructions.

For a single file, the supplied upload command uses XMODEM with CRC:

    UPLOAD MYPROG.LBR

Supply the intended CP/M filename, then choose XMODEM/CRC send in the terminal
and select your local file. For a batch, use:

    UPLOAD /Y

and choose YMODEM send at your end. Follow the prompts and wait for
confirmation. XMODEM may leave record padding at the end of the received file;
YMODEM carries its declared length.

Your submission goes into the sysop's private review inbox. It won't
immediately appear in the public library or in WORK, and DIR cannot list that
inbox. The sysop will examine it before deciding whether to add it to a
collection.

It's helpful to accompany an upload with a note giving its filename, purpose,
author, supported hardware and any distribution restrictions. Use MESSAGE or a
suitable RBBS message to contact the sysop.

A completed file survives a disconnect. An unfinished partial upload is
discarded; a completed member of a YMODEM batch is retained even if a later
member fails.

The fact that a file was uploaded successfully is not a promise that it will
be published or made executable on the board.
